    "result_text": "Clean build passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test green). Here's how I addressed each finding.\n\n## Finding 2 \u2014 duplicated household traversal (agreed, fixed)\nExtracted `HouseholdMembers.of(owner, repository)` as the single definition of \"household member\" (other non-deleted owners sharing the `householdId`, self excluded). All three consumers now delegate to it:\n- `DeriveOwnerHouseholdSize` \u2192 `1 + members.size()`\n- `CapOwnerMembershipLevel` \u2192 max level over `members`\n- `DetectOwnerPossibleDuplicate` \u2192 earliest member by id\n\nThis also fixed a real lockstep drift the finding warned about: `DeriveOwnerHouseholdSize` previously counted soft-deleted owners while the duplicate checks excluded them. They're now unified on \"exclude soft-deleted\" \u2014 the coherent meaning, and behaviour-neutral for the acceptance test (no deleted members there).\n\n## Finding 1 \u2014 removal of `checkHouseholdUnique` (partially disagree, and cleaned up)\nI can't do the literal ask (restore the step): the acceptance test creates a second owner in the same household with no `sharesHousehold` flag and expects 2xx, so re-adding the 409 block fails the test. This checkpoint's rule deliberately **replaces** the hard \"one owner per household \u2192 409\" reject with the soft membership-level cap \u2014 the spec's whole premise (\"maximum membershipLevel among their household members\") requires households to hold multiple members.\n\nBut the reviewer's underlying concern \u2014 half-deleted rule left as orphaned dead code \u2014 is valid, and my pipeline change had already made several javadocs stale (they still claimed household joins are \"rejected 409\"). So rather than leave it dangling, I removed the superseded rule **completely** and made the cleanup explicit:\n- Deleted `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique`, `DuplicateHouseholdException`, `DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler`, and the escalation YAML (all served only this rule \u2014 no other references).\n- Updated the now-stale javadocs in `AssignOwnerHousehold`, `DetectOwnerPossibleDuplicate`, and `Owner` (`possibleDuplicate` + `identityKey`) to reflect the new reality: a non-declared household join is allowed, soft-flagged as a possible duplicate, and level-capped.\n\nOne consequence worth flagging: a second household member is now flagged `possibleDuplicate = true` (previously it was rejected before reaching that flag). No test pins that field, and it's consistent with the new \"joins are allowed but suspect\" model. I left `openapi.yml`'s documentation of the old 409 untouched \u2014 editing that generated contract file is outside this change's scope; say the word if you'd like it reconciled too.\n\n(Note: the first re-run failed only because Maven had kept a stale compiled copy of the deleted YAML in `target/classes`; a `clean` build resolved it and is green.)",
